    {'heading': 'Lesson XXXII'},
    {'para': '**337. Comparison of Adjectives.** Derivative adjectives having comparative and superlative meaning — or often, and more originally, a merely intensive value — are made either (A.) directly from roots (by primary derivation), or (B.) from other derivative or compound stems (by secondary derivation).'},
    {'para': '**338. A.** The suffixes of primary derivation are ⟨īyas⟩ for the comparative, and ⟨iṣṭha⟩ for the superlative. The root before them is accented, and usually strengthened by *guṇa* (if capable of it), or sometimes by nasalization or prolongation. — In classical Sanskrit few such formations are in use; and these attach themselves in meaning mostly to other adjectives from the same root, which seem to be their corresponding positives. In part, however, they are connected with words unrelated to them in derivation.'},
    {'para': '**339.** Thus ⟨kṣepīyas⟩ and ⟨kṣepiṣṭha⟩ (root ⟨kṣip⟩) attach themselves to ⟨kṣipra⟩ ‘quick’; ⟨varīyas⟩ and ⟨variṣṭha⟩ (root ⟨vṛ⟩ ‘encompass’), to ⟨uru⟩ ‘broad’; ⟨pāpīyas⟩ and ⟨pāpiṣṭha⟩, ‘worse’ and ‘worst’, to the subst. ⟨pāpa⟩; ⟨paṭīyas⟩ and ⟨paṭiṣṭha⟩, to ⟨paṭu⟩ ‘skilful’; ⟨mahīyas⟩ and ⟨mahiṣṭha⟩ to'},
    {'para': '⟨mahant⟩; ⟨balīyas⟩ and ⟨baliṣṭha⟩, to ⟨balin⟩ or ⟨balavant⟩; ⟨sādhīyas⟩ and ⟨sādhiṣṭha⟩ to ⟨sādhu⟩.'},
    {'para': '**340.** The following are examples of artificial connections: ⟨antika⟩ ‘near’, ⟨nedīyas⟩, ⟨nediṣṭha⟩; ⟨alpa⟩ ‘little’, ⟨kanīyas⟩, ⟨kaniṣṭha⟩ (but also ⟨alpīyas⟩, ⟨alpiṣṭha⟩); ⟨guru⟩ ‘heavy’, ⟨garīyas⟩, ⟨gariṣṭha⟩; ⟨dīrgha⟩ ‘long’, ⟨drāghīyas⟩, ⟨drāghiṣṭha⟩; ⟨praśasya⟩ ‘praiseworthy’, ‘good’, ⟨śreyas⟩ ‘better’, ⟨śreṣṭha⟩ ‘best’; ⟨priya⟩ ‘dear’, ⟨preyas⟩, ⟨preṣṭha⟩; ⟨bahu⟩ ‘much’, ⟨bhūyas⟩, ⟨bhūyiṣṭha⟩; ⟨yuvan⟩ ‘young’, ⟨yavīyas⟩, ⟨yaviṣṭha⟩; ⟨vṛddha⟩ ‘old’, ⟨varṣīyas⟩, ⟨varṣiṣṭha⟩. ⟨jyāyas⟩ and ⟨jyeṣṭha⟩ correspond sometimes to ⟨praśasya⟩ or ⟨sādhu⟩, sometimes to ⟨vṛddha⟩.'},
    {'para': '**341.** The stems in ⟨iṣṭha⟩ are inflected like ordinary adjectives in ⟨a⟩, with the fem. in ⟨ā⟩; those in ⟨īyas⟩ have a peculiar declension, with a strong stem in ⟨īyāṃs⟩, and fem. ⟨īyasī⟩, for which see § 255. So also ⟨jyāyas⟩ and ⟨bhūyas⟩.'},
    {'para': '**342. B.** The suffixes of secondary derivation are ⟨tara⟩ and ⟨tama⟩. They are of almost unrestricted use. That form of stem is usually taken which appears before an initial consonant of a case-ending. Stems in ⟨as⟩ are always unchanged; final ⟨vas⟩ and ⟨us⟩ become ⟨vaṣ⟩ and ⟨uṣ⟩, after which the ⟨t⟩ of the suffix becomes ⟨ṭ⟩.'},
    {'para': 'Thus, ⟨priyavāc⟩, ⟨priyavāktara⟩, ⟨-ktama⟩; ⟨dhanin⟩, ⟨dhanitara⟩, ⟨-tama⟩; ⟨vidvāṃs⟩, ⟨vidvattara⟩, ⟨-ttama⟩.'},
    {'para': '**343.** Some stems which are substantives rather than adjectives are found to form derivatives of comparison; thus, ⟨mātṛtama⟩ ‘most motherly’, ⟨nṛtama⟩ ‘most manly’, ⟨gajatama⟩ ‘most like an elephant.’'},
    {'para': '**344. Comparison of Adverbs.** Adverbs are compared by adding the suffixes in the forms ⟨tarām⟩ and ⟨tamām⟩; thus, ⟨su⟩ ‘well’, ⟨sutarām⟩, ⟨sutamām⟩.'},
    {'para': '**345. Construction.** With a comparative (and sometimes with other words used in a similar way) the ablative is the regular construction; thus, ⟨putrāt kanyā tasya preyasī⟩ “a daughter is dearer to him than a son”; ⟨matir eva balād garīyasī⟩ “intellect alone is'},
    {'para': 'stronger than force”. After the superlative either genitive or locative may be used. The comparative often has the force of a strengthened superlative; thus, ⟨garīyān⟩ ‘most honorable’.'},
